Article 26 Duty-related violations subject to investigation by supervisory organs refer to the following illegal acts committed by public officials that are related to their duties, which do not constitute crimes but for which they shall bear legal liability according to law:
(1) Illegal acts committed by abusing authority;
(2) Illegal acts committed by taking advantage of the influence of their positions;
(3) Illegal acts of ineffective performance of duties, dereliction of duty, or failure to fulfill responsibilities;
(4) Other illegal acts that violate specific obligations related to the duties of public officials.

Article 27 Where a supervisory organ discovers that a public official has committed other illegal acts, and any of the following circumstances exists, it may conduct investigation and handling according to law:
(1) The statute of limitations for administrative violation has expired, or the statute of limitations for criminal prosecution has expired and criminal liability has not been pursued, but an administrative sanction needs to be imposed according to law;
(2) Administrative legal liability has been pursued, and an administrative sanction needs to be imposed according to law;
(3) When investigating a duty-related violation or duty-related crime, the supervisory organ simultaneously verifies other illegal acts committed by the investigated person that are simple and clear in facts and for which an administrative sanction needs to be imposed according to law.
Where a supervisory organ discovers that a public official committed illegal acts as prescribed in the preceding paragraph before becoming a subject of supervision, it shall handle the matter in accordance with the preceding paragraph.

Article 39 Where a supervision organ, based on the results of supervision and investigation, discovers that the unit to which the supervisee belongs has any of the following circumstances requiring rectification and correction, it shall, according to law, make a supervision proposal to promote the work of learning from cases and promoting reform:
(1) There are prominent problems in the construction of clean government;
(2) There are significant risks in the restriction and supervision of the exercise of power;
(3) There are prominent problems in the education, management, and supervision of supervisees;
(4) The implementation of laws, regulations, and systems is not up to standard;
(5) There is failure to perform or incorrect performance of statutory duties;
(6) Other circumstances where a supervision proposal is necessary.
The supervision organ shall follow up and understand the adoption of the supervision proposal, guide and urge the relevant unit to make rectification within a time limit, and propose further rectification opinions for those that have not met the rectification requirements, so as to ensure the implementation of the supervision proposal.

Article 43 The management personnel of state-owned enterprises as referred to in Item 3 of Article 15 of the Supervision Law refer to the following personnel in state-invested enterprises:
(1) Personnel who perform duties such as organization, leadership, management, and supervision in wholly state-owned or wholly state-funded companies or enterprises;
(2) Personnel who, through nomination, recommendation, appointment, or approval by Party organizations or state organs, wholly state-owned or wholly state-funded companies or enterprises, or public institutions, perform duties such as organization, leadership, management, and supervision in state-controlled or state-invested companies and their branches;
(3) Personnel who, with the approval or decision after study by the organization responsible for the management and supervision of state-owned assets in state-invested enterprises, engage in organization, leadership, management, supervision and other work on its behalf in state-controlled or state-invested companies and their branches.

Article 45 The management personnel in primary-level self-governing mass organizations as referred to in Item 5 of Article 15 of the Supervision Law refer to the following personnel in such organizations:
(1) Personnel engaged in the management of collective affairs and public welfare undertakings;
(2) Personnel engaged in the management of collective funds, assets, and resources;
(3) Personnel who assist the people's government in performing administrative management work, including the management of relief funds and materials for disaster relief, epidemic prevention, emergency rescue, flood control, special care, assistance, resettlement, and relief; the management of funds and materials for social donation to public welfare undertakings; the operation and management of state-owned land; the management of compensation fees for land expropriation and requisition; the collection and payment of taxes on behalf of the government; work related to family planning, household registration, and conscription; and other management work in primary-level self-governing mass organizations assisting the people's government and other state organs.

Article 46 The following persons are other personnel who perform public duties according to law as referred to in Item 6 of Article 15 of the Supervision Law:
(1) Deputies to the people's congresses at all levels who perform the duties of the people's congresses, members of the committees of the Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference at all levels who perform public duties, people's assessors, and people's supervisors;
(2) Personnel who, although not included in the staffing of Party and government organs, engage in public affairs in Party and government organs;
(3) Personnel who, through nomination, recommendation, appointment, or approval by Party organizations or state organs, wholly state-owned or wholly state-funded companies or enterprises, organizations responsible for the management and supervision of state-owned and collective assets in state-invested enterprises, or public institutions, engage in organization, leadership, management, supervision and other work in collective economic organizations and other units or organizations;
(4) Personnel who, in bidding evaluation, negotiation, inquiry and other organizations established according to law, temporarily perform duties such as organization, leadership, management, and supervision of public affairs on behalf of state organs, wholly state-owned or wholly state-funded companies or enterprises, public institutions, or people's organizations;
(5) Other personnel who exercise public power according to law.

Article 50 A superior supervisory organ may, according to law, elevate the level of jurisdiction over duty-related illegal or criminal cases within the jurisdiction of a lower-level supervisory organ under any of the following circumstances:
(1) Having a significant impact within its jurisdiction;
(2) Involving supervisees under the jurisdiction of multiple lower-level supervisory organs, making investigation difficult;
(3) Other major or complex cases requiring elevation of jurisdiction.
A superior supervisory organ may, when necessary, directly investigate, or organize, direct, or participate in the investigation of, any case with a significant impact within the jurisdiction of any supervisory organ under its supervision, according to law.
Where a duty-related illegal or criminal case under the jurisdiction of a local supervisory organ at any level meets the circumstances specified in paragraph 1, that organ may, according to law, request a supervisory organ at the next higher level to assume jurisdiction.

Article 51 A superior supervisory organ may, according to law, designate a case under its own jurisdiction to a lower-level supervisory organ for jurisdiction.
Where a supervisory commission of a city divided into districts designates to a lower-level supervisory commission a case involving a public official under the management of the same-level Party committee suspected of duty-related illegality or crime, it shall report to the provincial-level supervisory commission for approval; where a provincial-level supervisory commission designates such a case to a lower-level supervisory commission, it shall submit the matter to the relevant supervision and inspection department of the National Commission of Supervision for filing.
Where a superior supervisory organ, with respect to a duty-related illegal or criminal case under the jurisdiction of a lower-level supervisory organ, finds it more appropriate for another lower-level supervisory organ to assume jurisdiction under any of the following circumstances, it may, according to law, designate the case to that other lower-level supervisory organ for jurisdiction:
(1) There is a dispute over jurisdiction;
(2) Designation of jurisdiction is conducive to the fair handling of the case;
(3) The lower-level supervisory organ requests designation of jurisdiction;
(4) Other circumstances where designation of jurisdiction is necessary.
The designated lower-level supervisory organ shall not redesignate the case for jurisdiction without the approval of the supervisory organ that made the designation. If it discovers new leads of duty-related illegality or crime, or other important circumstances or major issues, it shall promptly report to the supervisory organ that made the designation for instructions.

Article 63 Where a supervisory organ takes a supervisory measure and, according to law, requires the presence of a witness, it shall invite a suitable witness to be present. The following persons shall not serve as witnesses:
(1) Persons who have physical or mental defects or are minors and lack the corresponding ability to discern or correctly express themselves;
(2) Persons who have an interest in the case and may affect the fair handling of the case;
(3) Staff members of the supervisory organ or persons employed by it;
(4) Staff members who assist the supervisory organ in taking supervisory measures according to law.

Article 66 All materials that can be used to prove the facts of a case are evidence, including:
(1) Physical evidence;
(2) Documentary evidence;
(3) Witness testimony;
(4) Victim statements;
(5) Statements, confessions and exculpations of the person under investigation;
(6) Expert evaluation opinions;
(7) Records of inspection and examination, identification, investigation experiments, etc.;
(8) Audio-visual materials and electronic data.
When collecting or obtaining evidence from relevant units or individuals, a supervisory organ shall inform them that they must provide evidence truthfully according to law. For those who fail to provide relevant materials as required, disclose relevant information, forge, conceal or destroy evidence, provide false information, or prevent others from providing evidence, legal liability shall be pursued according to law.
Evidentiary materials collected by a supervisory organ in accordance with the Supervision Law and these Regulations may be used as evidence in criminal proceedings if they meet the statutory requirements after review.

Article 69 For a duty-related illegal case that a supervisory organ has concluded its investigation, the facts shall be clear and the evidence shall be conclusive. Evidence is conclusive if it meets the following conditions:
(1) All facts concerning the characterization and disposition are supported by evidence;
(2) The evidence on which the case is decided is truthful and lawful;
(3) There are no irresolvable contradictions among the evidence relied upon to decide the case;
(4) Considering all the evidence in the case, the facts determined are clear and convincing.

Article 70: For a duty-related crime case that the supervisory organ has concluded investigation, the facts shall be clear and the evidence shall be reliable and sufficient. Evidence being reliable and sufficient shall meet the following conditions:
(1) All facts for conviction and sentencing are proved by evidence;
(2) The evidence on which the case is determined have all been verified as true through statutory procedures;
(3) Comprehensive evaluation of all evidence in the case has eliminated reasonable doubt regarding the facts determined.
If the evidence is insufficient, the case shall not be transferred to the People's Procuratorate for examination and prosecution.

Article 97: If a witness, expert evaluator, or victim, due to testifying, faces danger to the personal safety of himself, herself, or his or her close relatives, and requests protection from the supervisory organ, the supervisory organ shall accept the request and conduct a timely review; if there is indeed a danger to personal safety, the supervisory organ shall take necessary protection measures. If the supervisory organ discovers the existence of the above circumstances, it shall proactively take protection measures.
The supervisory organ may take one or more of the following protection measures:
(1) not disclosing personal information such as real name, address, and work unit;
(2) prohibiting specific persons from contacting the witness, expert evaluator, victim, and their close relatives;
(3) taking special protection measures for persons and residences;
(4) other necessary protection measures.
Where it is decided according to law not to disclose the personal information such as the real name, address, and work unit of a witness, expert evaluator, or victim, pseudonyms may be used in legal documents such as inquiry records and evidentiary materials. However, a separate written explanation of the use of pseudonyms shall be made, the classification level shall be indicated, and it shall be kept in a separate volume.
If the supervisory organ needs assistance in taking protection measures, it may request assistance from public security organs and other relevant units and require relevant individuals to provide assistance according to law.

Article 106: Ordered awaiting investigation shall be executed by the supervisory organ that decided to take the measure.
The supervisory organ executing the ordered awaiting investigation shall perform the following duties:
(1) supervise and inspect the person subject to ordered awaiting investigation to comply with relevant provisions, and promptly keep abreast of his or her activities, address, work unit, contact information, and changes thereof;
(2) examine and approve applications by the person subject to ordered awaiting investigation to leave the city urban area of the municipality directly under the Central Government or the city with districts where he or she resides, or the jurisdiction of the city without districts or county where he or she resides (hereinafter collectively referred to as the city or county of residence);
(3) promptly stop or rectify violations by the person subject to ordered awaiting investigation of the provisions he or she shall comply with;
(4) jointly with the work unit and family members of the person subject to ordered awaiting investigation, carry out ideological education and psychological counseling for the person.

Article 108: If a person subject to ordered awaiting investigation needs to leave the city or county of residence, he or she shall submit a written application to the supervisory organ, specifying the reason, destination, route, means of transportation, date of departure and return, contact information, etc. The supervisory organ shall make a decision within three days from the date of receipt of the written application. If the person subject to ordered awaiting investigation has an urgent reason and cannot submit a written application in a timely manner, he or she may first submit the application by telephone or other means, and promptly complete the written application formalities afterwards.
After the supervisory organ approves the application for the person subject to ordered awaiting investigation to leave the city or county of residence, it shall inform him or her to comply with the following requirements:
(1) keep contact information open, and promptly appear for investigation upon receiving notice;
(2) travel strictly in accordance with the approved location, route, and dates of departure and return;
(3) not engage in activities that hinder the investigation;
(4) report to the executing organ promptly after returning to the place of residence.
For a person subject to ordered awaiting investigation who needs to frequently leave the city or county of residence due to normal work or life needs, the approval procedures may be simplified according to the circumstances, and one-time approval may be granted for him or her to travel in accordance with the approved location and route within a specified period.

Article 109: A person subject to ordered awaiting investigation who falls under any of the following circumstances may be found to have violated the provisions on ordered awaiting investigation as specified in Article 23, Paragraph 3 of the Supervision Law, and the circumstances are serious:
(1) attempting to escape or commit suicide;
(2) engaging in conduct such as forging, concealing, or destroying evidence, or colluding to make false statements, or interfering with witness testimony, seriously affecting the normal progress of the investigation;
(3) retaliating against relevant persons such as informants, accusers, victims, witnesses, or expert evaluators;
(4) leaving the city or county of residence without approval, seriously affecting the normal progress of the investigation, or leaving the city or county of residence without approval on two occasions;
(5) failing to appear after being notified without justifiable reasons, seriously affecting the normal progress of the investigation, or failing to appear after being notified without justifiable reasons on two occasions;
(6) changing the address, work unit, or contact information, etc., and failing to report to the supervisory organ as required, resulting in inability to notify the person to appear, seriously affecting the normal progress of the investigation.
Where a person subject to ordered awaiting investigation in accordance with Article 23, Paragraph 1, Item 3 of the Supervision Law violates the provisions on ordered awaiting investigation with serious circumstances, and should be detained according to law, the provincial-level supervisory organ shall report to the National Commission of Supervision for approval, and supervisory organs at the level of a city with districts or below shall report level by level to the provincial-level supervisory organ for approval.

Article 122 Where a person under investigation falls under any of the following circumstances, he or she may be found to be likely to escape or commit suicide as prescribed in Article 24, paragraph 1, item 2 of the Supervision Law:
(1) He or she has begun to prepare for suicide, self-harm, or escape;
(2) He or she has previously attempted suicide, self-harm, or escape;
(3) He or she has the intention to commit suicide, self-harm, or escape;
(4) Other circumstances indicating a likelihood of escape or suicide.

Article 123 Where a person under investigation falls under any of the following circumstances, he or she may be found to be likely to collude or to falsify, conceal, or destroy evidence as prescribed in Article 24, paragraph 1, item 3 of the Supervision Law:
(1) He or she has previously attempted or is attempting to collude, or to falsify, conceal, destroy, or transfer evidence;
(2) He or she has previously attempted or is attempting to intimidate, threaten, induce, or bribe witnesses, or to interfere with witness testimony;
(3) A co-offender or an involved person who is closely related to the person under investigation in illegal or criminal activities is at large, and important evidence has not yet been collected;
(4) Other circumstances indicating a likelihood of collusion or falsification, concealment, or destruction of evidence.

Article 124 Where a person under investigation falls under any of the following circumstances, he or she may be found to be likely to engage in other acts that obstruct the investigation as prescribed in Article 24, paragraph 1, item 4 of the Supervision Law:
(1) He or she may continue to commit illegal or criminal acts;
(2) There is a real risk of endangering national security or public safety;
(3) He or she may retaliate against whistleblowers, accusers, victims, witnesses, expert evaluators, or other relevant persons;
(4) He or she, without justifiable reasons, refuses to appear before the supervisory organ, seriously affecting the investigation;
(5) Other acts that may obstruct the investigation.

Article 125 No detention measure shall be taken against the following persons:
(1) Those suffering from a serious illness and unable to take care of themselves;
(2) Women who are pregnant or are breastfeeding their own infants;
(3) The sole supporter of a person who is unable to take care of himself or herself.
After the above circumstances cease to exist, a detention measure may be taken against the relevant person as required by the investigation.

Article 129 The period of detention shall not exceed three months, commencing from the date of announcement to the person under detention. Under any of the following circumstances, the period may be extended once after approval, and the extension shall not exceed three months:
(1) The case is of great significance, seriously harming national interests or public interests;
(2) The case is complex, involving many persons involved, a huge amount of money, and a wide scope;
(3) Important evidence has not yet been collected, or important persons involved have not yet appeared before the organ, necessitating continued investigation into the main facts of the illegal or criminal act;
(4) Other circumstances requiring an extension of the detention period.
Where a supervisory organ below the provincial level takes a detention measure, an extension of the detention period shall be submitted to the supervisory organ at the next higher level for approval.
Where the detention period is extended, the decision to extend the detention period shall be announced to the person under detention before the expiration of the original period, and the person shall be requested to sign and affix a fingerprint on the Decision on Extending the Detention Period. If the person under detention refuses to sign or affix a fingerprint, the investigation personnel shall record it on the instrument.
Where the detention period is extended, the unit and family members of the person under detention shall be notified promptly.

Article 174 A record of identification shall not be used as the basis for determining a case under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The identifier was allowed to see the identification target before the identification began;
(2) The identification activity was not conducted individually;
(3) The identification target was not mixed with other objects with similar characteristics, or the number of objects provided for identification does not meet the requirements, except for a specific identification target;
(4) Obvious suggestions were given to the identifier during the identification, or there is obvious suspicion of leading;
(5) The identification was not conducted under the direction of an investigator;
(6) Other circumstances in which the authenticity of the record of identification cannot be determined due to violation of relevant provisions.
If the record of identification has other defects, its authenticity and relevance shall be examined in light of all the evidence in the case, and a comprehensive judgment shall be made.

Article 176 The supervisory organ may conduct the following expert evaluations according to law:
(1) Evaluation of handwriting, printed documents, damaged documents, documents of unknown creation time, and documents presented in other forms;
(2) Accounting evaluation of financial accounting materials and related property involved in the case;
(3) Psychiatric evaluation of the capacity for conduct of the person under investigation or witnesses;
(4) Medical evaluation of personal injury or death for damage to the human body or cause of death;
(5) Evaluation of audio and video recording materials;
(6) Electronic data evaluation of materials arising from the application of electronic information technology and their derivatives;
(7) Other professional evaluations that may be conducted according to law.

Article 180 A supplementary evaluation shall be conducted upon review under any of the following circumstances:
(1) There is a clear omission in the evaluation content;
(2) New evidence objects of significance for evaluation are discovered;
(3) There are new evaluation requirements for the evaluated evidence objects;
(4) The evaluation opinion is incomplete, and the entrusted matters cannot be determined;
(5) Other circumstances requiring supplementary evaluation.

Article 181 A re-evaluation shall be conducted upon review under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The evaluation procedure is illegal or violates relevant professional and technical requirements;
(2) The evaluation institution or evaluator does not have the qualification and conditions for evaluation;
(3) The evaluator intentionally makes a false evaluation or violates the rules of recusal;
(4) The basis for the evaluation opinion is clearly insufficient;
(5) The materials to be examined are false or damaged;
(6) Other circumstances requiring re-evaluation.
If a re-evaluation is decided, a different evaluation institution and evaluator shall be designated.

Article 201: A supervisory organ shall promptly review problem leads transferred by law enforcement organs, judicial organs, or other organs, and handle them in the following manner:
(1) If the organ has jurisdiction, it shall promptly study and propose disposal opinions;
(2) If the organ does not have jurisdiction but another supervisory organ does, it shall transfer the leads to the supervisory organ with jurisdiction within five working days;
(3) If the organ has jurisdiction over part of the problem leads, it shall propose disposal opinions regarding the part within its jurisdiction and promptly transfer the other problem leads to the organ with jurisdiction;
(4) If no supervisory organ has jurisdiction, it shall promptly return the leads to the transferring organ.

Article 225 The case adjudication department may, based on the adjudication circumstances and after approval, have a conversation with the person under investigation, inform him/her of the rights and obligations during the adjudication stage, verify the facts of the suspected illegal or criminal conduct, hear his/her defense opinions, and learn about the relevant circumstances. The conversation with the person under investigation shall be conducted in a place with safety and security conditions. If the person under investigation is a criminal suspect or defendant in custody or a person serving a sentence in a detention center or prison, the matter shall be handled in accordance with Article 83 of these Regulations. During the conversation, there shall be no fewer than two case adjudication personnel.
Where any of the following circumstances exists, a conversation with the person under investigation shall generally be held:
(1) The person under investigation is subject to custodial measures and is to be transferred for prosecution;
(2) There may be circumstances where evidence is collected by illegal methods;
(3) The person under investigation signs a dissenting opinion on the materials of the suspected illegal or criminal conduct or refuses to sign;
(4) The person under investigation requests to make an oral statement to the case adjudication personnel;
(5) Other circumstances where it is necessary to have a conversation with the person under investigation.

Article 235 Where a supervisory organ, in accordance with law, issues a supervisory recommendation to the unit to which the supervisee belongs, it shall, upon approval, produce a written supervisory recommendation.
A written supervisory recommendation shall generally include the following:
(1) the situation of supervision and investigation;
(2) the main problems discovered and their causes;
(3) the content and requirements of the rectification recommendation;
(4) the time limit for rectification and the requirements for reporting the rectification status; and
(5) the time limit and method for raising objections.

Article 245 Where a person under investigation suspected of duty crimes meets any of the following circumstances and truthfully confesses his/her main criminal facts, he/she may be deemed to have voluntarily surrendered and sincerely repented and shown remorse as specified in item (1) of Article 34 of the Supervision Law:
(1) Surrendering to the supervisory organ before the supervisory organ has knowledge of the duty crime issue or while the supervisory organ is making appropriate inquiries into the relevant leads;
(2) During the process of talks or correspondence inquiries by the supervisory organ, truthfully confessing to suspected duty crime issues not known to the supervisory organ;
(3) Surrendering during the preliminary verification stage before being summoned for a talk by the supervisory organ;
(4) Although the duty crime issue has been placed on file by the supervisory organ, surrendering before being interrogated or subjected to supervisory compulsory measures;
(5) Due to objective reasons such as illness or injury, being unable to surrender in person and first entrusting another person to express the intention to surrender, or expressing the intention to surrender by letter, internet, telephone, fax, etc., and later appearing before the supervisory organ for handling;
(6) Surrendering after fleeing while suspected of duty crimes, including surrendering during the process of being wanted or arrested;
(7) It is verified that the person was indeed preparing to surrender, or was apprehended by the relevant authorities while on the way to surrender;
(8) Surrendering upon persuasion by others or accompanied by others;
(9) Although not surrendering to the supervisory organ, surrendering to the Party organization, unit, or relevant responsible persons of the unit, surrendering to the relevant inspection and tour inspection agencies, or surrendering to the public security organ, the People's Procuratorate, or the People's Court; or
(10) Having other circumstances that shall be deemed as voluntary surrender.
If, after voluntary surrender, the person under investigation fails to truthfully confess his/her main criminal facts, or after voluntarily surrendering and truthfully confessing his/her crimes, later retracts the confession, the provisions of the preceding paragraph shall not apply.

Article 246 Where a person under investigation suspected of duty crimes meets any of the following circumstances, he/she may be deemed to have actively cooperated with the investigation and truthfully confessed to illegal or criminal acts not yet known to the supervisory organ as specified in item (2) of Article 34 of the Supervision Law:
(1) The criminal facts targeted by the leads known to the supervisory organ are not established, and the person under investigation voluntarily confesses to other crimes beyond that scope;
(2) Voluntarily confessing to criminal facts not yet known to the supervisory organ, which are of a different type from the criminal facts already known to the supervisory organ;
(3) Voluntarily confessing to criminal facts not yet known to the supervisory organ, which are of the same type as the criminal facts already known to the supervisory organ; or
(4) The evidence held by the supervisory organ is insufficient, and the truthfully confession by the person under investigation helps in collecting evidence for the final determination of the case.

Article 248 Where a person under investigation suspected of duty crimes meets any of the following circumstances, he/she may be deemed to have performed major meritorious service as specified in item (4) of Article 34 of the Supervision Law:
(1) Reporting and exposing a major criminal act committed by another person, which is verified to be true;
(2) Providing important leads for other major cases, which are verified to be true;
(3) Preventing another person from committing a major criminal act;
(4) Assisting in the arrest of other persons under investigation for major duty crime cases or major criminal suspects (including accomplices); or
(5) Having made other significant contributions to the state and society, such as saving the state from major losses.
For the purposes of the preceding paragraph, "major crime" generally refers to a criminal act that may be punishable by fixed-term imprisonment of life imprisonment or above according to law; "major case" generally refers to a case that has a relatively significant impact within the province, autonomous region, municipality directly under the Central Government, or nationwide; "verified to be true" generally refers to a situation where the relevant case has been placed on file for investigation or investigation by the supervisory organ or judicial organ, the person under investigation or criminal suspect has been subjected to supervisory compulsory measures by the supervisory organ or criminal compulsory measures by the judicial organ, or the defendant has been convicted by the People's Court, and such determination is made in combination with the facts and evidence of the case.
The circumstances specified in item (4) of Article 34 of the Supervision Law where a case involves major national interests refer to situations where the case involves national sovereignty and territorial integrity, national security, foreign affairs, social stability, economic development, etc.

Article 256: The supervisory organ shall cooperate with the People's Procuratorate in respect of the following requirements raised in writing by the People's Procuratorate during the review of prosecution:
(1) Where it is believed that there may be evidence collected by illegal means and the People's Procuratorate requests the supervisory organ to provide explanations or provide relevant evidentiary materials regarding the legality of evidence collection;
(2) Where after excluding illegal evidence, the People's Procuratorate requests the supervisory organ to assign other investigators to re-collect evidence;
(3) Where there are doubts about physical evidence, documentary evidence, audio-visual materials, electronic data, and records of inspections, examinations, identifications, investigation experiments, etc., and the People's Procuratorate requests the investigator to provide information regarding the acquisition and production;
(4) Where the People's Procuratorate requests the supervisory organ to conduct an expert evaluation on certain specialized issues in the case, or to re-examine or re-check the inspection and examination;
(5) Where the People's Procuratorate believes that the main facts of the crime have been ascertained but some evidence still needs to be supplemented or improved, and requests the supervisory organ to supplement and provide evidence;
(6) Other work requirements raised by the People's Procuratorate in accordance with law.

Article 258: Where a case is returned by the People's Procuratorate for supplementary investigation, after approval, the following actions shall be taken respectively:
(1) If the evidence for determining the criminal facts is insufficient, after supplementing the evidence, a supplementary investigation report shall be prepared and transferred together with the relevant materials to the People's Procuratorate for review. For evidence that cannot be supplemented or improved, a written explanation shall be made and stamped with the seal of the supervisory organ or the responsible department;
(2) If new accomplices are discovered or criminal facts are added or changed during the supplementary investigation and criminal liability needs to be investigated, a new handling opinion shall be proposed and transferred to the People's Procuratorate for review;
(3) If there is a significant change in the determination of criminal facts and it is believed that the person under investigation should not be investigated for criminal liability, a new handling opinion shall be proposed within the time limit for supplementary investigation, and the handling result shall be notified in writing to the People's Procuratorate with reasons given;
(4) If it is believed that the criminal facts transferred for prosecution are clear and the evidence is reliable and sufficient, reasons shall be stated and the case shall be transferred to the People's Procuratorate for review according to law.

Article 265: The National Commission of Supervision shall coordinate and plan international anti-corruption exchanges and cooperation with other countries, regions, and international organizations.
The National Commission of Supervision shall organize the implementation of the United Nations Convention against Corruption and other international anti-corruption treaties as well as the compliance review, and shall undertake the relevant work of the central authority for mutual legal assistance under the United Nations Convention against Corruption.
The National Commission of Supervision shall organize and coordinate relevant units to establish a centralized, unified, efficient, and smooth coordination mechanism for international anti-corruption fugitive repatriation and asset recovery and prevention of flight, and shall coordinate, supervise, and guide the handling of foreign-related cases such as international anti-corruption fugitive repatriation and asset recovery by supervisory organs at all levels, specifically performing the following duties:
(1) Formulating work plans for international anti-corruption fugitive repatriation and asset recovery and prevention of flight, and studying important issues in the work;
(2) Cooperating with relevant units in handling major foreign-related cases such as international anti-corruption fugitive repatriation and asset recovery;
(3) Handling foreign-related cases within the jurisdiction of the National Commission of Supervision;
(4) Guiding local supervisory organs at all levels in handling foreign-related cases according to law;
(5) Summarizing and reporting information on duty crime fugitive cases nationwide and information on fugitive repatriation and asset recovery work;
(6) Establishing and improving the cooperation network for international anti-corruption fugitive repatriation and asset recovery and prevention of flight;
(7) Undertaking the duties of the competent authority for international criminal judicial assistance carried out by supervisory organs;
(8) Undertaking other duties related to the handling of foreign-related cases such as international anti-corruption fugitive repatriation and asset recovery.

Article 266: Local supervisory organs at all levels, under the leadership of the National Commission of Supervision, shall coordinate, supervise, and guide the handling of foreign-related cases such as international anti-corruption fugitive repatriation and asset recovery in their respective regions, and specifically perform the following duties:
(1) Implementing the work arrangements of the supervisory organ at the higher level regarding international anti-corruption fugitive repatriation and asset recovery and prevention of flight, and formulating work plans;
(2) Handling foreign-related cases in accordance with the jurisdictional authority or the jurisdiction designated by the supervisory organ at the higher level;
(3) In accordance with the requirements of the supervisory organ at the higher level, assisting and cooperating with other supervisory organs in handling foreign-related cases;
(4) Summarizing and reporting information on duty crime fugitive cases in the region and information on fugitive repatriation and asset recovery work;
(5) Undertaking other duties related to the handling of foreign-related cases such as international anti-corruption fugitive repatriation and asset recovery in the region.
Provincial supervisory commissions shall, together with relevant units, establish and improve the coordination mechanism for international anti-corruption fugitive repatriation and asset recovery and prevention of flight in the region.
Dispatched or delegated supervisory agencies and commissioners of the National Commission of Supervision shall coordinate, supervise, and guide the handling of foreign-related cases such as international anti-corruption fugitive repatriation and asset recovery by the units to which they are dispatched, and shall follow the provisions of the first paragraph by reference.

Article 281 Where illegal gains and other property involved in the case located overseas shall be recovered according to law, the supervisory organ shall order the persons involved to return or make restitution in a lawful manner. If the persons involved refuse to do so, the supervisory organ may recover them according to law through the following methods:
(1) When conducting cooperation in fugitive pursuit such as extradition, additionally request the relevant country (region) to transfer the relevant illegal gains and other property involved in the case;
(2) Initiate the confiscation procedure for illegal gains according to law, apply to the People's Court for a confiscation order concerning the relevant illegal gains and other property involved in the case, and request the relevant country (region) to recognize and enforce the order and return the property;
(3) Request the relevant country (region) to recover the relevant illegal gains and other property involved in the case according to law and return them;
(4) Recover through other lawful methods.

Article 286 The supervisory organ at each level shall, through channels such as internet government media, newspapers, radio, and television, promptly and accurately disclose to the public the following supervision work information:
(1) Supervision regulations;
(2) Case investigation information that shall be disclosed to the public according to law;
(3) Information on the address, telephone, and website for reporting and accusing;
(4) Other information that shall be disclosed according to law.

Article 293: The supervisory organs and the heads of their supervision and inspection departments and investigation departments shall regularly inspect the audio and video recordings, conversation records, interrogation records, inquiry records, and registration materials of the property involved in the case during the investigation period, strengthen full-process supervision over the investigation, and promptly rectify any problems found and report them.
For the synchronous audio and video recordings of conversations, interrogations, and inquiries, the focus shall be on checking whether the following circumstances exist:
(1) Collecting evidence by illegal methods such as violence or threats;
(2) Failing to ensure the respondent's meals and necessary rest time;
(3) The start and end times recorded in the conversation records, interrogation records, or inquiry records are inconsistent with the start and end times reflected in the audio and video recordings of the conversations, interrogations, or inquiries;
(4) There are material differences between the contents of the conversation records, interrogation records, or inquiry records and the contents of the audio and video recordings of the conversations, interrogations, or inquiries.

Article 304: Where a supervisory organ, based on the facts and evidence already obtained, finds that a supervisory personnel suspected of serious duty-related violations or crimes may commit any of the following acts, it may, after approval according to law, impose confinement measures on such personnel in a venue with safety and security conditions:
(1) Continuing to commit illegal or criminal acts;
(2) Tipping off the respondent or persons involved in the case, thereby disclosing the secrets of supervision work;
(3) Threatening, intimidating, or deliberately retaliating against reporters, accusers, victims, witnesses, expert evaluators, and other relevant persons;
(4) Other acts that may cause more serious consequences or adverse effects.

Article 314: Where a relevant unit refuses to execute any of the following handling decisions lawfully made by a supervisory organ, the competent department or the superior organ shall order it to make corrections, circulate a notice of criticism against the unit, and handle the responsible leading personnel and the directly responsible personnel according to law:
(1) a decision on a disciplinary sanction for government affairs;
(2) a decision on accountability;
(3) a decision on interview reminders, criticism education, ordering a check, or admonishment;
(4) a decision on taking investigative measures;
(5) a decision on review or re-examination;
(6) other handling decisions lawfully made by the supervisory organ.

Article 318: Where a supervisor commits any of the following acts in performing duties, he or she shall be seriously dealt with according to law; if a crime is constituted, criminal liability shall be investigated according to law:
(1) engaging in corruption, bribery, favoritism, or malpractice;
(2) failing to perform or correctly perform supervision duties, failing to discover problems that should have been discovered, or failing to report or handle problems discovered, causing serious impact;
(3) disposing of problem leads without approval or authorization, concealing major case situations without reporting, or privately retaining or handling case-related materials;
(4) using his or her powers or the influence of his or her position to interfere with investigation work;
(5) unlawfully stealing or disclosing investigation information, or disclosing reporting matters, the acceptance of reports, or the identities of informants;
(6) extorting confessions or inducing confessions from the person under investigation or involved persons, or insulting, beating, abusing, physically punishing, or covertly physically punishing them;
(7) disposing of sealed, seized, or frozen property in violation of regulations;
(8) causing a case-handling safety accident or incident due to violation of regulations, or concealing, making false reports, or improperly handling such accident or incident after its occurrence;
(9) taking measures of compulsory appearance, ordered awaiting investigation, custodial care, detention, or confinement in violation of regulations, or failing to lift or change such measures after the expiration of the statutory time limit;
(10) taking technical investigation measures or exit restriction measures in violation of regulations, or failing to lift technical investigation measures or exit restriction measures as prescribed;
(11) unlawfully interfering with the production or operation of enterprises or infringing upon the personal rights, property rights, or other lawful rights and interests of business operators by using his or her powers;
(12) other acts of duty-related illegality or duty-related crime.

Article 320: Where a supervisory organ or its staff member has any of the following circumstances when exercising its functions, the victim may apply for state compensation:
(1) unlawfully taking custodial care or confinement measures, or taking such measures in accordance with statutory conditions and procedures but exceeding the statutory time limit for custodial care or confinement, and subsequently deciding to dismiss the case;
(2) deciding to dismiss the case after taking detention measures;
(3) unlawfully confiscating, recovering, sealing, seizing, or freezing property, causing damage;
(4) unlawfully exercising functions, causing bodily injury or death to the person under investigation, involved persons, or witnesses;
(5) other acts infringing upon the lawful rights and interests of citizens, legal persons, or other organizations, causing damage.
Where the victim dies, his or her successors and other family members who have a dependency relationship shall have the right to claim compensation; where the aggrieved legal person or other organization is terminated, its successor in interest shall have the right to claim compensation.

Article 323: For the purposes of these Regulations, the term "serious duty-related illegality" refers to a situation where, based on the facts and evidence already obtained by the supervisory organ, the duty-related illegal act suspected of being committed by the person under investigation is serious and may result in a disciplinary sanction for government affairs of removal from office or above.
For the purposes of these Regulations, the term "major duty-related crime" or major crime such as serious corruption or bribery refers to a duty-related crime that has any of the following circumstances:
(1) the case is major and complex, involves national interests or major public interests, or the criminal act causes particularly heavy losses to public property, the state, or the interests of the people;
(2) the person under investigation may be sentenced to fixed-term imprisonment of 10 years or more;
(3) the case has a significant impact nationwide or within the province, autonomous region, or municipality directly under the Central Government.
